// server/prisma/schema.prisma
//
// The blind relay's storage.  ADR 003 §5.1 as refined by LZP-201; addendum §3; decision D2.
//
// REGION: eu-central-1 (Frankfurt).  Vercel spells the same datacentre `fra1` and pins it in
// server/vercel.json; Prisma Postgres spells it `eu-central-1`. Neither name is a typo for the
// other, both are Frankfurt, and the Datenschutz copy (21.3) names the region — so changing
// either one without changing that copy makes the privacy text false. Decision D2.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT THIS SCHEMA CAN SEE — the whole list, and it is short
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// space ids and kinds; pseudonymous member ids; member colour refs; join and removal times;
// device ids, shorts and PUBLIC keys; per-device read/write progress; epoch numbers and rotation
// times; which device deposited each key wrap; per-space op counts; padded envelope sizes; op
// ARRIVAL times; chain hashes; invite id hashes, epochs and expiry.
//
// Nothing else of a family's board. No entry text, no bar caption, no category, no scratchpad,
// no date, no display name, no authoring time. Story 21.1.
//
// AND, SINCE THE LZP-1009 SECOND PASS, ONE THING THAT IS NOT A BOARD: the `Report` table at the
// bottom of this file holds the sentence somebody typed into „Rückmeldung senden", verbatim and
// in the clear, for 90 days. That sentence is READ by the operator — it is the entire product —
// so this paragraph's "nothing else" is about the family's board and always was. The report
// table can be joined to nothing here: it has no spaceId and no relation, which is what keeps a
// report off the family's board structurally. Read its own header before adding a column to it.
//
// THE STRUCTURAL RULE, not a convention: every ciphertext-bearing column below is `Bytes`, and
// `server/core/store-interface.js` REFUSES a String in any of them at the adapter boundary. The
// column set is closed and is compared field-by-field against MODEL_COLUMNS by
// `tests/server/store-contract.test.js`, so a new column cannot arrive here without arriving
// there too — which is the only way "a future handler cannot store a readable note" stays true
// after this file stops being read.
//
// TWO deliberate plaintext Strings that are not ids, and they are different in kind:
//   · `Member.colorRef` — story 15.3 needs `@@unique([spaceId, colorRef])` server-side to stop
//     two members choosing the same colour, and that check cannot be done inside the ciphertext.
//     It reveals one palette index per member and it appears verbatim in the Datenschutz copy.
//   · `Report.prose` — her sentence, verbatim, because the operator READS it. This one is not a
//     side effect of a constraint; it is the point of the table. It arrived with the LZP-1009
//     second pass, `tests/server/blindness.test.js` §2 is where the second row was forced to be
//     a decision rather than a diff, and the Datenschutz copy (21.3) states it and its 90 days.
//
// TWO COLUMNS THE §3 SKETCH HAD AND THIS DOES NOT:
//   · `Member.displayNameCipher` — display names travel as `member.set` ops inside the encrypted
//     stream, so the relay holds strictly less.
//   · `Op.ts` — authoring time lives inside the ciphertext (ADR 002 §5.1). The relay keeps
//     `receivedAt`, which it cannot avoid knowing.
// And one the ADR 002 §7.1 sketch had: `Invite.wrappedKeys`, removed by PO decision D9. An
// invite carries no key material of any kind. There is no column to put it in.

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

enum SpaceKind {
  PERSONAL
  FAMILY
}

/// One family circle or one person's own space. `nextSeq` is the whole of ADR 003 §3.3.
model Space {
  id           String    @id
  kind         SpaceKind
  currentEpoch Int       @default(1)
  /// RULE 2 — the per-space, row-locked, GAPLESS counter. Bumped by
  /// `UPDATE "Space" SET "nextSeq" = "nextSeq" + $n WHERE id = $1 RETURNING "nextSeq"`.
  /// NEVER a Postgres SERIAL: a global sequence leaves gaps when transactions commit out of
  /// order, and `WHERE seq > cursor` can then skip an op that committed after a higher-numbered
  /// one. It also leaks cross-family activity volume, which a per-space counter does not.
  nextSeq      BigInt    @default(0)
  /// ADR 002 §5.4 — the chain head. Opaque; diagnostic only.
  headChain    Bytes?
  createdAt    DateTime  @default(now())
  /// WHO CREATED THIS SPACE.  Finding T5-M1a.
  ///
  /// Written by the relay inside `POST /spaces`, from the Member row it creates in the same
  /// transaction. No route sets it, no body field reaches it, and it NEVER moves afterwards —
  /// so nobody claims it, and it is a historical fact the relay observed rather than a role.
  ///
  /// WHY IT EXISTS. Removing this member purges ADR 001 §4.0's attestation and §4.1's admin
  /// genesis link from the relay, after which `adminAtIn` answers null for every stamp in the
  /// space and no future joiner can admit anything the founder ever wrote. It is the one removal
  /// that destroys the space for everybody rather than for one person, so it — and only it —
  /// requires a second member's recovery signature (ADR 003 §3.7).
  ///
  /// WHAT IT IS NOT: an admin column. The admin chain is an in-log `transferAdmin` op the relay
  /// cannot read (ADR 001 §4.1), so the relay still cannot name the current admin, and this
  /// column must never be read as if it could. Nullable because every Space row created before
  /// this column existed has no founder recorded, and a null must fail OPEN (an ordinary
  /// removal) rather than wedge those spaces.
  founderMemberId String?

  members Member[]
  ops     Op[]
  invites Invite[]
  epochs  Epoch[]
  wraps   KeyWrap[]
}

/// Pseudonymous. There is no name column and no role column: the admin is resolved from the
/// in-log admin chain (ADR 001 §4.1), never from a row the relay could edit.
model Member {
  id             String    @id
  spaceId        String
  /// THE ONE DELIBERATE PLAINTEXT LEAK — see the header. One palette index per member.
  colorRef       String
  /// 65-byte raw P-256 point. Verifies device attestations and POST /devices/adopt (ADR 002 §7.3).
  recoveryPubSig Bytes
  /// 65-byte raw P-256 point (RK_kex). A rotation wraps the epoch key to every device AND to
  /// each member's recovery key (ADR 002 §4.2 step 2); without this column that wrap had no
  /// public key to address. Closes finding E3-2.
  recoveryPubKex Bytes
  joinedAt       DateTime  @default(now())
  removedAt      DateTime?

  devices Device[]
  space   Space     @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([spaceId, colorRef])
  @@index([spaceId, removedAt])
}

/// A device is a machine. `deviceShort` is the identity (ADR 001 §1.2 derives it from the
/// signing key); `id` is a LABEL bound by nothing, and ADR 002 §2.3 forbids keying anything on
/// it alone. That is why the op purge below is keyed on `deviceShort`.
///
/// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// THE `deviceShort` NAMESPACE IS PER SPACE — round 10 item 8, closing E2-203-1
/// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// It used to be `deviceShort String @unique`, globally. That was wrong in two directions and
/// the second one is worse than the product bug that found it:
///
///   1. It BLOCKED THE PRODUCT. `IK_sig` is minted per DEVICE (ADR 002 §2.1), so a Mac has one
///      `deviceShort` for life, while `Device.memberId` points at exactly one `Member`, which is
///      per space. One Mac could therefore have a row in exactly ONE space — and 19.4 (personal)
///      plus 15.2 (family) require it to be in two. Finding E2-203-1.
///   2. IT MADE AN UNFORGEABLE IDENTITY INTO A CLAIMABLE NAME. `deviceShort` is a *function of a
///      public key*, and `GET /spaces/:id/members` publishes `sigPubRaw` to every member of the
///      space (`DEVICE_PROJECTION`). So any member of your circle could compute your short,
///      register a row carrying it under her own member — `POST /devices` is authorized by an
///      attestation she signs with her OWN recovery key, and P2 only checks that the short is
///      derived from the presented key, which it is — and thereby take the ONE global slot your
///      Mac would ever have. One request, and that Mac can never create a personal space or join
///      another circle again. A uniqueness constraint on a value everyone can compute is not an
///      identity check; it is a land grab with a queue.
///
/// The fix is the namespace, not a new check: `@@unique([spaceId, deviceShort])`. A squatted row
/// is now local to the squatter's own circle and inert there — she cannot AUTHENTICATE as it
/// (ADR 003 §2 step 5 needs the private key she does not have), and it denies the owner nothing
/// anywhere else. The adversary's other option, "require a signature by the key being
/// registered", is already enforced everywhere it can be (auth.js's bootstrap binding, and step
/// 4b + step 5 on every later request); the one door it is NOT on is `POST /devices`, which is
/// deliberately unauthenticated. See ADR 003 §5.1 for the residual that leaves and who owns it.
///
/// `spaceId` is DERIVED, not new knowledge: the relay already resolved a device's space through
/// `Device.memberId -> Member.spaceId`, and `addDevice` refuses a row whose `spaceId` disagrees
/// with its member's. It is here because the constraint needs a column to name, and because
/// `(spaceId, deviceShort)` as the leading pair means a bare `WHERE deviceShort = ?` is not
/// index-served. That is a speed bump for ad-hoc SQL and NOT a control against the operator, who
/// can build any index they like — and who has `sigPubRaw`, a strictly better join key, anyway.
/// The cross-space correlation this unblocks is stated in `docs/v2/server-metadata.md` §7.
model Device {
  id           String    @id
  /// The namespace of `deviceShort`. See the note above. Must equal `member.spaceId`.
  spaceId      String
  memberId     String
  deviceShort  String
  sigPubRaw    Bytes
  kexPubRaw    Bytes
  /// Signed by the owning member's recovery key. Opaque to the relay, which cannot verify it —
  /// the four acceptance conditions are a client-side fold (ADR 002 §2.3).
  attestation  Bytes
  /// READ progress: the highest seq this device has folded. Reported as `ackSeq` on push.
  lastSeenSeq  BigInt    @default(0)
  /// WRITE progress: the space's seq high-water when this device last confirmed a DRAINED
  /// outbox. Both gate tombstone GC, and read progress alone is unsafe — a device can be caught
  /// up on reads and still hold a three-week-old unpushed edit that GC would resurrect.
  lastPushedSeq BigInt   @default(0)
  addedAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  revokedAt    DateTime?

  member Member @relation(fields: [memberId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Round 10 item 8. NOT `@@unique([memberId, deviceShort])` — that was E2-203-1's own proposal
  /// and it is one notch too loose: two DIFFERENT members of the SAME circle could then register
  /// the same short, and ADR 003 §2 step 4 would have two rows to choose between inside one
  /// space with nothing to choose on. Per space, the resolution is exact.
  @@unique([spaceId, deviceShort])
  @@index([memberId])
}

/// The append-only log. `envelope` is iv || ct || sig, padded to a 256-byte bucket. UNREADABLE.
model Op {
  spaceId     String
  seq         BigInt
  /// 22 b64url. The idempotency key: `@@unique([spaceId, opId])` is what makes a lost response
  /// cost one retry and never a duplicate entry.
  opId        String
  epoch       Int
  /// Needed to purge a removed member's ops (20.2) and to check `e.dv === auth.deviceShort`.
  deviceShort String
  witness     Bytes?
  chain       Bytes
  envelope    Bytes
  /// ARRIVAL time. Not authoring time — that is inside the ciphertext and stays there.
  receivedAt  DateTime @default(now())

  space Space @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([spaceId, seq])
  @@unique([spaceId, opId])
  @@index([spaceId, seq])
  @@index([spaceId, deviceShort])
}

/// First-writer-wins rotation (ADR 002 §4.2). The composite primary key IS the race resolution:
/// a concurrent rotator gets a unique violation, which the handler answers as 409 epoch_taken.
model Epoch {
  spaceId   String
  epoch     Int
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  space Space @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([spaceId, epoch])
}

/// One epoch key, wrapped to one recipient. `wrapped` is {salt,iv,ct} and is opaque.
///
/// `recipientId` is deliberately NOT a foreign key onto Device. It holds either a device id
/// (`dev_…`) or the reserved recovery form `rec_<memberId>`, because ADR 002 §4.2 step 2 wraps
/// to each member's RK_kex as well as to each device, and a recovery recipient has no Device
/// row. Closes finding E3-4.
///
/// `senderDeviceId` is WHO DEPOSITED THE ROW, and it is written by the relay from the request it
/// authenticated — never read off the body. ADR 002 §4.2 step 6 makes the receiving device verify
/// who sent a wrap before it derives a KEK against it (finding S1), and `admitWraps` cannot do
/// that without an index into its own verified sender set. E2 had no column for it, so E3's
/// required `ctx.senders` parameter had nothing to select against and every honest wrap was
/// refused — finding E2E3-3.
///
/// Why a stamped device id rather than the ADR's client-supplied `senderKexPubRaw`: a 65-byte
/// client-chosen blob on every wrap row is a covert channel a blind relay has no reason to
/// accept, while `senderDeviceId` carries ZERO client-chosen bits and states no fact the relay
/// did not already observe — it authenticated that device on that request. `GET /keys` publishes
/// the ADR's `senderKexPubRaw` unchanged, by joining to `Device.kexPubRaw`, so the wire the
/// client reads is exactly the one §4.2 step 6 specifies.
///
/// Not a foreign key, for the same reason `recipientId` is not: the Device row can be cascaded
/// away by a member removal while the wrap it deposited is still being purged. A dangling sender
/// publishes as `null`, which `SenderSet.lookup` answers `null` for, which is `unauthorized` —
/// the correct outcome for a key deposited by a device this space no longer admits.
/// ── WRITE-ONCE.  Finding T5-K3, and the reason `@@id` below is a constraint and not a hint. ──
///
/// A row of this table is INSERTED once and thereafter only ever DELETED (ADR 002 §4.2 step 4,
/// a removed or revoked recipient). It is never UPDATED. No adapter issues an UPDATE against
/// `KeyWrap`: `memory.js#putKeyWraps` fills an empty cell, no-ops an identical re-post and
/// refuses a differing one; `prisma.js#putKeyWraps` reads the cells, inserts only the absent
/// ones, and passes `skipDuplicates` so that even a race leaves the first writer standing.
///
/// WHY. `POST /spaces/:id/epoch` admits any current member — ADR 002 §4.2 and decision D9, "any
/// member device, not only the admin's" — and a rotation legitimately names wraps for every
/// epoch 1..e+1. While this was an upsert, that meant ONE member could replace every wrap of
/// every recipient for every epoch below her own with bytes of her choosing, and the relay
/// recorded it as coverage, because coverage counts ROWS and the relay may not open a wrap.
/// Nobody online would notice: a member's ring is in her own `localStorage`. The readers who
/// have only this table are every future joiner, every device paired in tomorrow, and ADR 002
/// §7.3's A2 recovery — the one path that survives losing every device.
///
/// WHY THE DEPOSITOR IS PART OF THE CELL. `senderDeviceId` is in the `@@id` because write-once
/// on the narrower `(spaceId, epoch, recipientId)` traded one denial for another: a JOINER's
/// cells for epochs 1..e are all empty when she arrives, so whoever rotates first owns them for
/// ever and every honest re-delivery is refused by the store. Measured — she held one epoch, no
/// history, and her ops quarantined (finding T5-K1's residual). With the sender in the key the
/// honest wrap and the junk COEXIST: `admitWraps` walks a recipient's rows and admits the one
/// that opens, `assertCoverage` folds them into a Set of epoch numbers so duplicates cannot
/// inflate coverage, and T5-K3 stays closed because no depositor can address another's row.
/// The cost is rows rather than data: one row per depositing device per (epoch, recipient),
/// bounded by MAX_WRAPS = 1024 per rotation and by the epoch race each rotation must win.
///
/// WHAT ENFORCES IT, HONESTLY. `@@id([spaceId, epoch, recipientId, senderDeviceId])` makes the
/// cell unique, so an INSERT is the whole write and a second INSERT cannot land. It does NOT
/// stop an UPDATE: Postgres has no write-once column and this schema declares no trigger and no
/// grant. So the rule lives in the two adapters, and `STORE_CONTRACT_CASES` C34/C61/C63 are what
/// hold them to it — including on the day this schema first meets a real Postgres.
model KeyWrap {
  spaceId        String
  epoch          Int
  recipientId    String
  wrapped        Bytes
  senderDeviceId String

  space Space @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([spaceId, epoch, recipientId, senderDeviceId])
  @@index([recipientId])
}

/// An invite authorizes MEMBERSHIP and nothing else. It carries no key material — decision D9.
/// The raw code never reaches the server: `id` is HKDF(code,…,'id') and `verifier` is the hash
/// of HKDF(code,…,'verify').
model Invite {
  id        String    @id
  spaceId   String
  verifier  Bytes
  wrapSalt  Bytes
  epoch     Int
  createdBy String
  expiresAt DateTime
  usedAt    DateTime?
  revokedAt DateTime?

  space Space @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([spaceId, usedAt, revokedAt])
}

/// ADR 002 §6.3. `rid` is HKDF of the 60-bit pairing code, so the relay never learns the code.
/// The security argument is a 180-second TTL times a hard 5-attempt cap — `burnedAt` is a
/// TOMBSTONE rather than a delete, because a row that a replayed pair/offer could re-create
/// would reset the budget and make the code the security parameter after all.
model PairSession {
  rid       String    @id
  boxA      Bytes?
  boxB      Bytes?
  delivery  Bytes?
  attempts  Int       @default(0)
  expiresAt DateTime
  burnedAt  DateTime?

  @@index([expiresAt])
}

/// The replay window (ADR 003 §2 step 3). Swept lazily on write; Hobby has no cron.
model Nonce {
  deviceShort String
  nonce       String
  expiresAt   DateTime

  @@id([deviceShort, nonce])
  @@index([expiresAt])
}

/// Every rate limit in ADR 003 §6.1 rides on this one table: push/pull per device, invite
/// redemptions per IP per hour, pair sessions per member per hour, pair GETs per IP.
/// `key` is composed of a route plus a device short or an IP — never of content.
model RateBucket {
  key         String   @id
  count       Int
  windowStart DateTime
}

/// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
/// A REPORT — „Rückmeldung senden".  LZP-1009 second pass; PO decisions of 2026-09-04/05.
/// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
///
/// ── WHAT IS ABSENT, AND WHY THE ABSENCE IS THE DESIGN ────────────────────────────────────────
/// There is NO `spaceId`, NO relation to `Space`, and no column any other model can be joined to.
/// That is what keeps *"a report can never appear on the family's board"* STRUCTURAL rather than
/// careful — the same sentence `server/core/handlers/feedback.js` promise 1 has always made,
/// now about a table instead of a sink. `listOps` has no path here, `deleteSpace`'s cascade has
/// no path here, and a future handler that wanted to publish a report onto a board would have to
/// invent the relation first, in this file, where the tests would see it (Principle 10 — the
/// board is not a messenger; there is no reply path, ever).
///
/// The reverse also holds and is deliberate: a family that deletes its circle (story 20.4) does
/// NOT delete the reports its members sent. A bug report is not the circle's to withdraw, and it
/// is addressed to the operator rather than held on the family's behalf.
///
/// ── WHAT THIS TABLE CAN SEE, AND IT IS MORE THAN ANY OTHER ───────────────────────────────────
/// `prose` is HER SENTENCE, VERBATIM, IN THE CLEAR. It is the second readable String in the whole
/// store that is not an id — `Member.colorRef` is the other — and the first that is prose. It is
/// deliberate and it is unavoidable: the product is that the operator reads what she wrote. Every
/// consequence is written into the Datenschutz copy (21.3) rather than left to be inferred, and
/// `tests/server/blindness.test.js` §2 is where the decision was forced to be a decision rather
/// than a diff.
///
/// The column is called `prose` and not `text`, `body` or `message` on purpose: all three read as
/// "somewhere content may go", and `store-interface.js#FORBIDDEN_COLUMN_TOKENS` exists to keep a
/// column like that from arriving by good intentions. `receivedAt` is ARRIVAL, exactly as `Op`'s
/// is — when she wrote it is not the relay's business, and `authoredat` is a forbidden token.
///
/// ── 90 DAYS, AND WHO ENFORCES THEM ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// `expiresAt` is `receivedAt` + 90 days, STAMPED BY THE STORE (`store-interface.js`
/// REPORT_RETENTION_DAYS, `normalizeReportInput`) and never supplied by a caller, so no handler
/// can file a report that outlives the number in the German copy. The sweep is LAZY, on put and
/// on list, because Vercel Hobby has no cron (ADR 003 §10 weakness 2) and reports arrive rarely —
/// a write-only sweep would leave rows for months, and he reads when the ⚙ dot appears, so the
/// READ path is the real cadence. Expiry is enforced AT READ as well, the way `getPairSession`
/// and `consumeInvite` already are: a retention promise that only holds once somebody writes to
/// the relay is not a retention promise. `@@index([expiresAt])` is the `Nonce`/`PairSession`
/// pattern; `@@index([receivedAt])` serves the admin view's newest-first read.
///
/// ── THE SIXTH INFERENCE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// `devicePub` is a 65-byte raw P-256 point the client MINTED ITSELF and never enrolled, so it
/// proves continuity between two reports and nothing else (`handlers/feedback.js` PROVES /
/// PROVES_NOT). But it is byte-identical to `Device.sigPubRaw` when the same Mac is in a circle,
/// so a RETAINED report is joinable to a circle — one join names the member, her circle and her
/// household. That is new because retention is new, it is not defended against, the operator is
/// the intended reader, and it is stated as the sixth inference in `docs/v2/server-metadata.md`
/// §7 rather than discovered later.
model Report {
  /// 'rep_' + 22 b64url, minted by the relay. The admin view's handle for one row: what „Löschen"
  /// addresses, and the reason a re-put under a live id is refused rather than an overwrite.
  id         String    @id
  /// HER SENTENCE, VERBATIM. See the header. The one readable prose column in this schema.
  prose      String
  /// The redacted PNG — produced by re-rendering the board with NO GLYPH PATH IN THE PRODUCT AT
  /// ALL, and opaque here: the relay checks eight signature bytes at the door and never parses one.
  image      Bytes?
  signed     Boolean
  /// 65-byte raw P-256, self-minted, NEVER ENROLLED. Absent when the Mac could not sign — which
  /// is the report the endpoint most exists for („mein Schlüsselbund ist kaputt").
  devicePub  Bytes?
  /// ARRIVAL time. Not authoring time; that is hers and the relay is not told it.
  receivedAt DateTime  @default(now())
  /// receivedAt + 90 days. Stamped by the store, never by a caller.
  expiresAt  DateTime

  @@index([expiresAt])
  @@index([receivedAt])
}
